<!--article-->
<worksheet margin="1in" bottom="1.25cm">
    <title>Math 483 Homework 4</title>

            
	<objectives>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<p>
					Use continuous differentiability and the Dirichlet kernel to show that if a function is continuously differentiable on <m>[-\pi,\pi]</m>, then its Fourier series converges uniformly to the correct function. 
				</p>
			</li>
	  	</ul>
    </objectives>
<page>


<exercise>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Show that 
            <md>
                |S_Nf(x) - f(x)| = \left| \frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi} (f(x+\phi) - f(x))D_N(\phi)d\phi\right|.
            </md>
            
            
        </p>
    </statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Using the fact that <m>f</m> is has a continuous derivative, argue that
            <md>
                f(x+\phi) - f(x) = f'(x)\phi + E(x,\phi),
            </md>
            where <m>\frac{E(x,\phi)}{\phi}\to 0</m> as <m>\phi\to 0</m>. <em>Because the interval <m>[-\pi,\pi]</m> is compact, this convergence is uniform with respect to <m>x</m>. You can use this!</em> 
        </p>
    </statement>
</exercise>

<p>
    The previous exercise shows that 
    <md>
         |S_Nf(x) - f(x)| = \left| \frac{f'(x)}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}\phi D_N(\phi)d\phi + \frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}E(x,\phi) D_N(\phi)d\phi\right|.
    </md>
    Now we wish to show this quantity converges to zero uniformly as <m>N\to\infty</m>.
</p>

<exercise>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Argue that the first of the two integrals given above is zero. Hint: <m>D_N</m> is an even function.
        </p>
    </statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            Now we need to argue that the second integral converges to zero as <m>N\to\infty</m> independent of <m>x</m>. To do this, we need to use the intalisized fact in exercise 2. This implies that for any <m>\epsilon &gt; 0</m> there is some <m>\delta &gt; 0</m> such that if <m>|\phi|&lt; \delta</m>, then <m>|E(x,\phi)| &lt; \epsilon </m> for all <m>x\in[-\pi,\pi]</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
            Thus, we can split the integral into pieces.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Argue that
                <md>
                    \frac{1}{2\pi}|\int_{-\delta}^{\delta}E(x,\phi) D_N(\phi)d\phi\right|
                </md>
                
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
</exercise>

</page>
    



</worksheet>
